\details{
Where a word begins and ends in CJK text is a fact about a language, not
about Unicode, so it cannot be derived the way everything else in this
package is. It needs a dictionary and a statistical model, and which one is
right depends on the language and the corpus. \pkg{tidycjk} therefore
dispatches on a name rather than committing to one.

Two engines ship with the package.

\code{"icu"} is a real word segmenter. ICU carries dictionary-based break
iterators for Chinese and Japanese, and stringi carries ICU, so this costs
no dependency you have not already installed: a six-character Chinese
sentence comes back as its four words rather than as six characters.

\code{locale} is accepted and forwarded, but it does not select the dictionary.
ICU applies a single combined Chinese-Japanese word list to Han and kana
runs, chosen by the script of the text, so Chinese and Japanese segment
the same way under \code{"zh"}, \code{"ja"} or the session default. It returns
surface forms only, with no part of speech, lemma or user dictionary, and
its models are lighter than MeCab's or jieba's tuned ones. It is the right
starting point and not the last word.

\code{"character"} needs nothing at all: every CJK character becomes its own
token and runs of non-CJK text are split on whitespace. Whitespace is never
a token, the ideographic space U+3000 included, even though \code{\link[=has_cjk]{has_cjk()}}
counts it as CJK. It is character tokenisation rather than word
segmentation, and for Chinese it will cut two-character words in half. It
is a baseline, not an answer.
}
\section{The two engines do not tokenise punctuation alike}{
This matters when comparing counts, so it is worth stating rather than
leaving to be discovered. \code{"icu"} drops punctuation and symbols along with
whitespace; \code{"character"} keeps CJK punctuation as tokens, because those
code points are in blocks \code{\link[=cjk_blocks]{cjk_blocks()}} lists, and keeps a run of
non-CJK text whole up to the next space.

The upshot is that the same sentence yields different token counts, and
the gap is punctuation rather than a disagreement about where words end.
An emoji is dropped by \code{"icu"} and kept by \code{"character"} for the same
reason. Filter or compare accordingly.
}

\section{Registering another segmenter}{
For Japanese, \href{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=gibasa}{gibasa} binds
MeCab and is on CRAN; it gives part of speech and lemma, which \code{"icu"} does
not.

\href{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=jiebaR}{jiebaR}, which binds
\href{https://github.com/yanyiwu/cppjieba}{cppjieba}, is the usual choice for
Chinese. It was archived from CRAN on 2025-05-01, so it cannot be a
dependency of a CRAN package and \code{install.packages()} will not find it;
install it from source with
\code{remotes::install_github("qinwf/jiebaR")}. Once you have it, four lines
make it an engine:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{register_cjk_segmenter("jiebar", function(x, ...) \{
  worker <- jiebaR::worker(...)
  lapply(x, function(s) \{
    if (is.na(s)) return(NA_character_)
    if (!nzchar(s)) return(character(0))
    as.character(jiebaR::segment(s, worker))
  \})
\})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The same shape works for any segmenter you can call from R.
}

\section{The engine contract}{
An engine is any function taking \verb{(x, ...)} -- a character vector and the
dots from \code{\link[=cjk_segment]{cjk_segment()}} -- and returning a list the same length as \code{x},
each element a character vector of tokens. \code{NA} input should give
\code{NA_character_} and the empty string should give \code{character(0)};
\code{\link[=cjk_segment]{cjk_segment()}} checks the shape and complains if an engine breaks the
contract. A plain list is required: a data frame is a list too, but
\code{length()} on one counts columns rather than elements, so it is refused
rather than quietly mistaken for a list of tokens.
}

\section{Passing arguments to an engine}{
Anything in \code{...} goes to the engine, which is how you configure one. Name
those arguments so that they are not a prefix of an argument of the verb
itself: \code{...} comes after \code{engine} in \code{\link[=cjk_segment]{cjk_segment()}}, and after \code{data} and
\code{col} in \code{\link[=cjk_tokens]{cjk_tokens()}}, so R's partial matching claims a prefix of one of
those before the dots ever see it.

It is worth knowing because the result does not look like an
argument-matching problem. \code{cjk_tokens(df, text, "mine", c = 1)} matches \code{c}
to \code{col}, which pushes the bare \code{text} into \code{engine}, where it resolves to
\code{\link[graphics:text]{graphics::text()}} -- a function, so it is accepted as an engine -- and the
error you get is about plotting. Single letters and short prefixes are the
risk: \code{c}, \code{co}, \code{d}, \code{da}, \code{e}, \code{en}, \code{eng}. A longer name, or a closure
that captures the setting instead of passing it, avoids the question:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{register_cjk_segmenter("mine", function(x, ...) my_segmenter(x, cutoff = 1))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{What registering does, and does not, undo}{
A registration lasts for the rest of the session and there is no function
to remove one. Registering the same name again replaces it, which is the
way to correct an engine you got wrong.

A name that matches a built-in shadows it. That is deliberate -- it is how
you substitute your own tokeniser for \code{"character"} without this package
getting a say -- but it is worth knowing that \code{"character"} is a natural
name for an engine and taking it hides the built-in for the session, with
nothing in \code{\link[=cjk_segmenters]{cjk_segmenters()}} to show that anything changed. Pick a
distinct name unless shadowing is what you meant.
}

